  "boostWithdrawEffect": "Turning it off stops anything new being sent. It cannot recall what was already sent.",
  "tip_trackDatesHelps": "Noting your period dates in the app helps you see your own pattern and gives a clinician clear facts.",
  "tip_periodLongerThanSeven": "A period that lasts longer than 7 days is worth mentioning to a clinician.",
  "tip_bleedingBetweenPeriods": "Bleeding between periods is worth mentioning to a clinician, so the cause can be looked into.",
  "tip_irregularCheckIn": "Cycles shorter than 21 or longer than 35 days are called irregular. Mention this to a clinician, who can look at your dates with you.",
  "tip_stoppedThreeMonths": "A period that stops for more than three months without a clear reason is worth a clinician's check, whatever your age.",
  "tip_irregularWithOtherSigns": "Irregular periods together with other changes, such as weight gain, extra hair on your face, or dry or oily skin, are worth mentioning to a clinician.",
  "tip_periodPainSeeClinician": "Period pain that stops you doing your usual daily activities is a reason to see a clinician.",
  "tip_severePainUrgent": "If pelvic or period pain is severe or worse than usual and painkillers have not helped, ask for urgent medical advice.",
  "tip_symptomDiaryBeforeVisit": "Before a visit about period problems, a diary of your symptoms across the whole cycle gives your clinician a detailed picture of what happens and when.",
  "tip_heavyOrTired": "Periods that keep you from daily activities, or leave you tired or short of breath a lot, can be signs of heavy periods. A clinician can help.",
  "tip_pmsDiary": "Keeping a diary of your symptoms for at least two cycles helps a clinician see whether they follow your period.",
  "tip_headachesAroundPeriod": "Migraines that come regularly before or during your period are worth mentioning to a clinician. A diary next to your period dates helps.",
  "tip_urineInfectionSeeClinician": "If you think you may have a urine infection, it is important to see a clinician. A urine test can tell.",
  "tip_leakingUrineTellClinician": "If you leak urine, it is worth telling a clinician, whatever the type or cause.",
  "tip_cervicalScreeningTip": "WHO advises cervical screening every 5 to 10 years from age 30; local programmes vary. Ask your clinic whether you are due.",
  "tip_ttcOneYear": "If a year of trying has passed without a pregnancy, it is a good idea to see a clinician and have the common causes checked.",
  "tip_ttcAgeSixMonths": "At 35 or older, it is advised to see a clinician after six months of trying, not twelve.",
  "tip_ttcIrregularNoWait": "With irregular or no periods, it is worth talking to a clinician about trying to conceive without waiting a full year.",
  "tip_regularSexTakesTime": "Most couples where the woman is under 40 conceive within a year of regular sex every 2 to 3 days. It can take time.",
  "tip_testingTooEarly": "Testing very early can give a false negative. Follow the test's own instructions.",
  "tip_folicAcidBeforePregnancy": "Folic acid, a B vitamin, is advised from at least a month before pregnancy. Ask a clinician how to get enough.",
  "tip_talkBeforeTrying": "It is a good idea to talk to a clinician before trying to get pregnant. They can help you get your body ready.",
  "tip_tryingIsStressful": "Trying to conceive can be stressful. Counselling and support groups may help you cope; you can ask a clinician about them.",
  "tip_movementsChangeCallNow": "If your baby moves less than usual or the usual pattern changes, call your midwife or maternity unit at once, even in the middle of the night.",
  "tip_firstMovementsBy24": "You should start to feel your baby move between 16 and 24 weeks. If there has been no movement by 24 weeks, tell your midwife.",
  "tip_preeclampsiaSigns": "A severe headache that painkillers do not ease, vision problems, pain below the ribs or sudden swelling need checking at once. Call your maternity unit.",
  "tip_antenatalChecksMatter": "Blood pressure and urine checks at antenatal visits can find changes you cannot feel, so try to keep every appointment.",
  "tip_screeningVersusDiagnostic": "Two kinds of tests are offered to every pregnant woman: screening tests, which show a chance, and follow-up tests, which give a firmer answer. Ask which suit you.",
  "tip_glucoseScreening": "All pregnant women should be screened for gestational diabetes. Ask your midwife or doctor when yours is planned.",
  "tip_rhBloodGroup": "A simple blood test shows your blood type and Rh status, usually at your first antenatal visit. Ask what yours is and whether it matters for your care.",
  "tip_mainScanBetween18And21": "A detailed scan is usually done between 18 and 21 weeks. It checks your baby's growth and looks for certain conditions.",
  "tip_severeSicknessTellEarly": "If you are being sick often and cannot keep food down, tell your midwife or doctor as soon as you can, so they can help you keep fluids down.",
  "tip_itchTellMidwife": "Itching is common in pregnancy, but it can be the first sign of a liver condition called ICP. Tell your midwife or doctor so it can be checked.",
  "tip_pretermLabourSigns": "Signs of early labour include contractions every 10 minutes or more often, leaking fluid or bleeding, pelvic pressure and a low, dull backache. Contact your midwife, doctor or maternity unit.",
  "tip_tirednessInPregnancy": "Tiredness in pregnancy has many causes, and anaemia is one of them. Mention ongoing tiredness to your midwife or doctor.",
  "tip_lowMoodPerinatal": "Feeling low is common in pregnancy and after birth, and support is available. Tell your midwife or doctor, even if you only have some of the signs.",
  "tip_anxietyInPregnancy": "Anxiety in pregnancy can often be helped with talking therapy. Tell your midwife or doctor how you really feel.",
  "tip_warningSignsPregnancyAndYear": "Some warning signs need care at once, in pregnancy and for a year after birth: a headache that will not go away, vision changes, fever, trouble breathing, severe belly pain.",
  "tip_postnatalCheckSixToEight": "A postnatal check around 6 to 8 weeks after birth makes sure you feel well and are recovering. Write down your questions to take along.",
  "tip_painfulFeedsGetHelp": "If you are putting off feeds because breastfeeding hurts, get help from a lactation consultant.",
  "tip_tirednessAfterBirthThyroid": "Tiredness after birth has many causes. Thyroid inflammation affects about 1 in 20 women in the first year after birth, so mention lasting tiredness to a clinician.",
  "tip_bleedingUpToSixWeeks": "Spotting or bleeding like a period, on and off, can last up to six weeks after birth. Tell a clinician if you are worried.",
  "tip_babyBluesPassing": "Feeling down, anxious or irritable for a few days after birth is very common and usually passes within 2 weeks. If it lasts longer, tell a clinician.",
  "tip_periodChangesFirstSign": "A change in your periods is usually one of the first signs of perimenopause. They may come more or less often, and be heavier or lighter.",
  "tip_twelveMonthsMeansMenopause": "Menopause means 12 months without a period. In the years before, cycles often vary, with longer or shorter gaps.",
  "tip_bleedingAfterMenopause": "Bleeding after menopause, meaning 12 months with no period, is important to tell a clinician about, so the cause can be found.",
  "tip_hotFlushesTalkOptions": "If lifestyle changes are not enough for hot flushes, contact a clinician. They will talk through your symptoms, history and preferences.",
  "tip_boneStrengthMenopause": "Loss of bone density is more likely around menopause. Ask a clinician about bone health and what suits you.",
  "tip_drynessManyTreatments": "There are many treatments for vaginal dryness. It is worth asking a clinician what could suit you.",
  "tip_heartbeatAroundMenopause": "A faster or more noticeable heartbeat can be part of perimenopause and menopause. Note when it happens and tell a clinician.",
  "tip_lowMoodMenopause": "Low mood and anxiety can be part of the menopause transition. If they are hard to manage, talk to a clinician.",
